    {
      "id": "https://bencane.com/posts/2026-10-21-timeout-uncertain-outcome/",
      "url": "https://bencane.com/posts/2026-10-21-timeout-uncertain-outcome/",
      "title": "A timeout doesn’t mean an operation failed. It means you don’t know the outcome.",
      "content_html": "<p>A trap I see people fall into when implementing resiliency is assuming a timeout means something failed.</p>\n<p>Let’s imagine you’re buying some very expensive concert tickets online.\nObviously, you’re trying to catch the latest Taylor Swift tour.</p>\n<p>You’ve picked your seats, entered your information, and clicked checkout.\nThen the page times out…</p>\n<p>Do you immediately go back and buy the tickets again?\nProbably not.</p>\n<p>First, you check whether your card was charged.\nYou look for a confirmation email.\nMaybe you check your account to see if the tickets are there.</p>\n<p>Only after confirming nothing happened do you decide whether to try again.</p>\n<p>Now imagine the same scenario, but you’re just looking at tour dates.\nWhen the page times out, do you refresh?\nAbsolutely.</p>\n<p>So why is one safe to unthinkingly retry and the other isn’t?\nBecause one scenario has an operation that could be painful if performed twice.</p>\n<p>A timeout on a checkout page doesn’t mean the operation failed; it just means you don’t know the outcome.\nAnd when you don’t know the outcome, a blind retry is a terrible idea.</p>\n<h2>The Blind Retry Resiliency Trap</h2>\n<p>Blind retries are a trap I see teams fall into when trying to make their systems more resilient.\nThey treat all requests the same.</p>\n<p>Maybe they do so by adopting a retry library or configuring automatic retries in their service mesh.\nOr maybe they ask an AI coding agent to add retry logic to every outbound request.</p>\n<p>But some requests need more than a retry.</p>\n<p>If you’re querying for data and the request times out, retrying might be perfectly fine.\nBut what if you're posting a financial transaction?\nJust because your client timed out doesn’t mean the transaction failed.</p>\n<p>Before implementing retries, ask yourself what happens if the first attempt actually succeeded.</p>\n<h2>Sometimes You Need to Know What Happened</h2>\n<p>For requests that perform operations with side effects, you need a way to manage that uncertainty.\nMaybe you query the downstream system to determine whether the transaction completed before retrying.</p>\n<p>Or maybe the operation is idempotent.\nIf every attempt includes a transaction ID and the downstream system recognizes duplicates before performing the operation again, great—retry.</p>\n<p>The key is, the right approach depends on the use case and the implementation at hand.</p>\n<h2>Doing Nothing Can Be Just As Bad</h2>\n<p>It might be tempting to read my warnings above and think, “ok I just won’t retry operations that make changes.”</p>\n<p>But that can also cause problems.</p>\n<p>Imagine a point-of-sale system initiating a payment.\nYour request to the payment system times out, so you don’t know whether the payment succeeded.\nMeanwhile, the point-of-sale system also times out waiting for you and tells the end-user the transaction failed.</p>\n<p>But what if the payment actually succeeded?\nThe end-user may be charged for something they were told they didn’t purchase.</p>\n<p>Doing nothing doesn’t always restore the system to a correct state.</p>\n<h2>Resiliency Requires Correctness</h2>\n<p>Resiliency isn’t just about making sure something eventually completes.\nIt’s also about maintaining correctness when things fail in unexpected ways.</p>\n<p>Sometimes that means retrying.\nSometimes it means checking the current state before deciding what to do.\nAnd sometimes it requires a compensating action.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If a payment fails, maybe you need to cancel a reservation.</li>\n<li>If a payment outcome is uncertain, maybe you need to put a shipment on hold.</li>\n<li>If fulfillment fails after the customer was charged, maybe you need to refund the transaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These decisions require something a generic retry library, and often a simple AI prompt, doesn’t understand: business context.</p>\n<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>\n<p>Retry libraries, exponential backoff, circuit breakers, and infrastructure-level resiliency features are incredibly useful.</p>\n<p>But they don’t inherently understand what “correct” means for your business operations.\nThat often requires business logic specifically designed to reconcile or compensate when an outcome is uncertain.</p>\n<p>A timeout doesn’t mean an operation failed.\nIt means you don’t know the outcome.\nResilient systems need to handle uncertainty.</p>",
      "date_published": "2026-10-22T00:00:00+00:00",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Benjamin Cane",
          "url": "https://bencane.com"
        }
      ],
